-- BLC8 universal machine for closed programs, strict: 321 bits (41 bytes).
-- The IOCCC 2012 uni8.Blc is 344 bits (43 bytes); tromp/AIT's uni8.lam
-- compiles to 355. Same contract: read <M> from the bits of the leading
-- bytes, discard the rest of the last byte touched, return M applied to the
-- remaining bytes; a free variable in M diverges (base environment omega).
--
-- The design reads every bit through one reader, get (Codex, 2026-10-12),
-- instead of two inline reads with their own refills. A refill moves to the
-- next byte, and a byte always has eight bits, so the refilled read can never
-- meet nil again: get needs no recursion (Claude). The variable parse is the
-- old one: Scott-pair environment, re-entering int8 with the second bit
-- pushed back. Verified against the 344 by `blam golf check --io bytes`
-- (every closed program to 18 bits, with and without a data byte, plus random
-- bytes: 0 differ, 0 halting on one side only) and by nested
-- self-interpretation; see docs/classical/golf.md.
--
-- test with
-- cat id.Blc id.lam | ./blc run8 uni8.lam
\bytes.bytes (
let dbl = \x.x x
in
  (dbl (\self.
    (\get\int8\cont.get (\b.get (\c\tail.b
      (int8 (\exp.c
        (cont (\args\arg.exp (\z.z arg args)))
        (int8 (\exp2.cont (\args.exp args (exp2 args))))) tail)
      (c (cont (\args.args c) tail)
         (int8 (\var.cont (\args.var (args c)))
               (\f.f c tail))))))
    -- get k bits = k bit tail, reading the next byte when bits is nil
    (\k. (\h\bits. bits h (\bytes.bytes (\byte. byte h byte))) (\b\tail\ignored.k b tail))
    (self self))
    (\exp\bits.exp (dbl dbl))))
